{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ CONFIG A (everything on Bedrock, INCLUDING cfg.model): 15 calls, ZERO on Anthropic — but the\nheadline number overstates the coverage, so here is what it does and does not establish. ═══\n\nSecond half of the operator's experiment. Config B (classes Bedrock, `cfg.model` left bare-anthropic)\nfound the four bypass sites. Config A additionally points `cfg.model` at Bedrock, so a call that\nmerely INHERITS cfg now lands on Bedrock too. The two configurations answer different questions:\n\n* **Config B** — does every call honour the CLASS configuration? (found 4 that do not)\n* **Config A** — is any provider HARDCODED past configuration entirely? (found none)\n\nConfig A alone is necessary but NOT sufficient: it would have passed even with every bypass site\nunfixed, because a bypassing call inherits `cfg.model` and `cfg.model` is Bedrock. Anyone re-running\nthis later should run BOTH, or the result is a false clean.\n\n    workflow            calls   non-bedrock\n    review-plan            5         0\n    verify-completion      1         0\n    review                 1         0\n    review-code            8         0\n    ─────────────────────────────────────\n    TOTAL                 15         0        (and 0 FAILED calls in every run, so no rows are missing)\n\n**THREE OF THE FOUR BYPASS SITES WERE EXERCISED AND ALL WENT TO BEDROCK** — `ticket-digest`,\n`ticket-quality`, `code-novelty`. That is real evidence for the mechanism: they inherit `cfg.model`,\nso moving `cfg.model` moves them.\n\n**THE FOURTH, `overlap-judge`, DID NOT RUN IN CONFIG A — its zero is ABSENCE, NOT CORRECTNESS.**\nRecording this rather than letting the total imply coverage it does not have. Two probes tried to\nforce it and both failed to, which is itself the useful finding:\n * re-running `review-plan` on 03b0 (the ticket that produced 18 overlap calls in config B) made\n   **zero LLM calls of any kind** — a current attestation short-circuits the whole gate.\n * running `review-plan` on 00da, never reviewed before, made 5 calls and still no overlap-judge.\n\n**SETTLED FROM THE CODE INSTEAD, which is conclusive here.** `overlap/judge.py:judge_one` builds\n`RunRequest(config=cfg, reviewers=[\"overlap-judge\"], ...)` — raw `cfg`, no class — and\n`wire.py:overlap_findings` sets `cfg = config or LLMConfig.from_env(...)`. That is the IDENTICAL\nmechanism measured three times over in config A, so under `cfg.model = bedrock` it resolves to\nBedrock, and under a class-only Bedrock config it does not (which is exactly the 18 anthropic calls\nconfig B measured). No further run is needed to characterise it.\n\n**AND THE TRIGGER IS NOW KNOWN, for whoever writes the regression test.** `wire.py` runs the judge\nonly when BM25F retrieval returns candidates: `candidates = retrieve(...)` then\n`if not candidates: return []`. Candidates exclude the query's own graph\n(`graph.related_ticket_ids`). So the 18 calls happened because 03b0 had many unrelated-but-similar\nneighbours at that moment; afeb and 00da had none. **A test for this must construct the candidate\ncondition deliberately — it cannot rely on running a gate and hoping the judge fires.**\n\n**WHAT CONFIG A PROVES, stated exactly:** no provider is hardcoded past configuration in\nreview-plan, review, review-code or verify-completion. Combined with config B, the complete claim is:\n\"every call reaches Bedrock when everything is configured for Bedrock, but four of them get there by\ninheriting `cfg` rather than by honouring the class — so a class-only cutover still leaks.\""},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785623895167183001,"uuid":"17f8c08d-1cf4-43ae-8024-81ccce92e764"}